<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      Phase 13-08 (D-17): the forced-change flow needs a single-use, hashed, short-lived token, and
      password_reset_tokens already models exactly that — a hash, an expiry and a used-at stamp,
      behind one row-level-security policy.

      WHY THIS TABLE RATHER THAN A SECOND ONE. A credential-bearing token is a thing you want in as
      few places as possible. One table means one RLS policy to get right, one grant to auth_user,
      one expiry sweep to write when someone finally writes it, and one place to look when a token
      leaks. Two tables means two of each, and the second one drifts — which is the failure mode
      this whole phase's audit is about. The cost is a discriminator column, which is cheap and,
      unlike a second table, is enforced by the database.

      NAMING NOTE: changeset "auth-1.0.0-061-enable-rls-password-reset-tokens" already lives INSIDE
      060-create-password-reset-tokens.xml. Liquibase keys on (id, author, filename) so there is no
      collision, but the ids here are deliberately suffixed differently so a human reading
      DATABASECHANGELOG can tell the two apart.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-061-password-token-purpose-column" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="password_reset_tokens" columnName="purpose"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <!--
          defaultValue RESET, not NULL-then-backfill: every row that exists when this runs was
          written by PasswordResetService, so RESET is the true value for all of them and the
          default also makes the column safe for any in-flight insert from a not-yet-restarted
          instance. A NOT NULL column with a default is one statement in Postgres 11+; no rewrite.
        -->
        <addColumn tableName="password_reset_tokens">
            <column name="purpose" type="VARCHAR(20)" defaultValue="RESET">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
        <!--
          The check constraint is the point of the column. Without it "purpose" is a free-text field
          and a typo ('FORCED-CHANGE', 'forced_change') produces a token that can never be redeemed
          by anything — an outage that looks like a token-generation bug. With it, the typo fails at
          the INSERT, in the service that made it, with the offending value in the message.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            ALTER TABLE password_reset_tokens
                ADD CONSTRAINT ck_password_reset_tokens_purpose
                CHECK (purpose IN ('RESET', 'FORCED_CHANGE'));
        </sql>
        <!--
          Redemption always matches hash AND purpose together (a reset token presented at the
          forced-change endpoint must miss, and vice versa), so the index carries both. The existing
          hash-only index stays: it is what an operator greps with, and dropping it would be a
          behaviour change to the reset path for no gain.
        -->
        <createIndex tableName="password_reset_tokens" indexName="idx_password_reset_tokens_hash_purpose">
            <column name="token_hash"/>
            <column name="purpose"/>
        </createIndex>
        <rollback>
            <dropIndex tableName="password_reset_tokens" indexName="idx_password_reset_tokens_hash_purpose"/>
            <sql splitStatements="false">
                ALTER TABLE password_reset_tokens DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS ck_password_reset_tokens_purpose;
            </sql>
            <dropColumn tableName="password_reset_tokens" columnName="purpose"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      NO SECURITY DEFINER LOOKUP FUNCTION HERE, AND THE ABSENCE IS DELIBERATE.

      The problem it would have solved is real. password_reset_tokens is FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY on
      app.current_tenant_id, and both flows that redeem a token by hash — forgot-password confirm,
      and the forced change this plan adds — are PUBLIC: the caller has no JWT, so
      JwtAuthenticationFilter never populates TenantContext and TenantAwareDataSource sets no GUC.
      The policy therefore hides the row from the very lookup that has to find it. Measured on the
      live dev auth_db before this plan: a freshly issued, unused, unexpired reset token was refused
      as "Invalid or expired reset token" while the matching row was demonstrably present. The
      forgot-password flow had never once worked against a database that enforces row security. The
      whole integration suite passed regardless, because Testcontainers' Postgres user is a
      SUPERUSER and superusers bypass row security entirely.

      The obvious fix was to copy changeset 052's auth_lookup_refresh_tenant, which resolves a
      refresh session's tenant before the GUC can be set. That was written, applied to the live
      database, and then WITHDRAWN, because measuring it showed it did not work:

        auth_lookup_refresh_tenant         owner = postgres    rolbypassrls = true
        auth_lookup_password_token_tenant  owner = auth_user   rolbypassrls = false

      SECURITY DEFINER runs a function as its OWNER, and FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY subjects even the
      table's owner to the policy. 052 works only because some earlier migration run created it as a
      superuser; Liquibase today runs as auth_user, so the identical DDL produced a function that
      returned NULL for a row that was right there. Depending on that would make a credential flow
      hinge on which account happened to run a migration — and it means the REFRESH path is one
      clean re-provision away from breaking the same way. That is recorded in 13-08-SUMMARY.md for
      whoever owns deployment; it is not this changeset's to fix, and 052 is left untouched.

      The token instead carries its own tenant routing prefix (PasswordPolicyService.generateToken),
      which needs no privileged database object, costs no isolation — the stored hash is of the
      whole token, so editing the prefix matches nothing anywhere — and cannot be broken by who runs
      the migration.
    -->

</databaseChangeLog>
